      <td class="content-column" id="content-area">
        <table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
          <tr>
            <td class="title-area">
              <a class="title" href=
              "/pjm/1985/117-1/pjm-v117-n1-p10-s.pdf">Derivations and
              Cayley derivations of generalized Cayley-Dickson algebras</a>

              <h3>Kevin Mor McCrimmon</h3>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>
              <div class="page-numbers">
                Vol. 117 (1985), No. 1, 163–182
              </div>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>
              <div class="paper-doi">
                DOI: <a href="http://dx.doi.org/10.2140/pjm.1985.117.163">10.2140/pjm.1985.117.163</a>
              </div>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </table>

        <table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" class="article">
          <tr>
            <td class="article-area">
              <h5>Abstract</h5>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td class="article-area">
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
            <!-- Generic HTML abstract--><p class = "indent">The Cayley-Dickson doubling
process can be continued past the quaternions and octonions to obtain an infinite
series of algebras of dimension <span 
class="cmr-10">2</span><sup><span 
class="cmmi-7">n</span></sup>. After <span 
class="cmmi-10">n </span><span 
class="cmr-10">= 3 </span>these algebras are no longer
composition algebras. R. D. Schafer established the surprising result that the
derivation algebras stop growing at <span 
class="cmmi-10">n </span><span 
class="cmr-10">= 3</span>. Schafer&#x2019;s proof assumed the scalars were a
field of characteristic <span 
class="cmmi-10">&#x2260;</span><span 
class="cmr-10">2</span><span 
class="cmmi-10">,</span><span 
class="cmr-10">3</span>. In this paper we will give a different proof of his result
which works for arbitrary rings of scalars, making use of the concept of a Cayley
derivation.</p>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </table>

